  "solution_body": "We prove the weakened best-known statement and the monotonicity-padding principle. Let $P\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ be finite, $|P|=n$, and let $U(P)$ be the number of unordered pairs $\\{p_1,p_2\\}\\subset P$, $p_1\\ne p_2$, with $|p_1-p_2|=1$. Define $U_{\\max}(n)=\\max_{|P|=n} U(P)$. The maximum exists since $0\\le U(P)\\le \\binom{n}{2}$. We claim no exact formula, no $\\Theta$-tightness, and no matching exponent. The provable best-known result is: there exist absolute constants $c>0$, $C>0$, $c'>0$ and $n_0$ such that for all $n\\ge n_0$,\n$$ c\\, n^{1+c'/\\log\\log n} \\le U_{\\max}(n) \\le C\\, n^{4/3}. $$\nIn particular the upper exponent $4/3$ and lower exponent $1+\\Omega(1/\\log\\log n)$ do not match; there is a large open gap. We require $n$ large so that $\\log\\log n>0$, e.g. $n>e^{e}$, and constants are independent of $n$ and $P$. This is an upper bound and a lower-bound construction, not a determination of $U_{\\max}(n)$.\n\nLemma (Monotonicity with isolated padding). $U_{\\max}(n)$ is nondecreasing in $n$. Moreover, given any $P$ with $|P|=n$, there exists $q\\notin P$ with $|q-p|\\ne 1$ for all $p\\in P$, so that for $P'=P\\cup\\{q\\}$, $|P'|=n+1$ and $U(P')=U(P)$. Hence $U_{\\max}(n+1)\\ge U_{\\max}(n)$.\n\nProof. Adding a point cannot destroy existing unit pairs: if $P\\subset P'$ then any unit pair of $P$ remains a unit pair of $P'$, so $U(P')\\ge U(P)$. Hence $U_{\\max}$ is nondecreasing trivially. For the isolated padding, let $S=P\\cup\\bigcup_{p\\in P}S^1(p)$ where $S^1(p)=\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}^2:|x-p|=1\\}$ is the unit circle centred at $p$. $P$ is finite and each $S^1(p)$ is a $1$-dimensional circle of Lebesgue measure zero; a finite union of such sets cannot cover $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Indeed $\\mathbb{R}^2\\setminus S$ is nonempty and in fact co-bounded: choose $q$ with $|q|$ larger than $\\max_{p\\in P}|p|+2$, then $q\\notin P$ and $|q-p|>1$ for all $p\\in P$, in particular $|q-p|\\ne 1$. More generally any $q\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\setminus S$ works. Then $P'=P\\cup\\{q\\}$ has $n+1$ distinct points, the pairs among $P$ are unchanged, and $q$ participates in $0$ unit pairs by construction, so $U(P')=U(P)$. Taking $P$ optimal for $n$, i.e. $U(P)=U_{\\max}(n)$, gives $U_{\\max}(n+1)\\ge U(P')=U_{\\max}(n)$. By induction $U_{\\max}$ is monotone.\\qed\n\nCorollary (Reduction to squares). To prove the lower bound $U_{\\max}(n)\\ge c\\,n^{1+c'/\\log\\log n}$ for all large $n$, it suffices to prove it for square cardinalities $n=m^2$. Indeed, given $n$, let $m=\\lfloor\\sqrt{n}\\rfloor$, so $m^2\\le n<(m+1)^2$ and $m^2\\to\\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty$. If $U_{\\max}(m^2)\\ge c\\,(m^2)^{1+c'/\\log\\log(m^2)}$ for large $m$, then by monotonicity $U_{\\max}(n)\\ge U_{\\max}(m^2)$. Since $m^2\\ge n/4$ for large $n$ (in fact $m^2=n-O(\\sqrt{n})$), the transfer to $n$ loses only an absolute constant factor and a negligible change in $\\log\\log$, absorbed by slightly shrinking $c,c'$. Explicitly, padding the $m^2$-point extremal example with $n-m^2$ far-away points chosen inductively outside all previous unit circles preserves the count. Hence lower-bound constructions may assume $|P|=m^2$ on an $m\\times m$ grid and then pad. No claim is made that padding is optimal or that far points never create new unit pairs in general; we choose them to create none, giving a safe lower bound.",
