  "solution_body": "We prove the weakened best-known form and the monotonicity-padding principle. Define for finite $P\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$, $|P|=n$, $U(P)$ as the number of unordered pairs $\\{p_1,p_2\\}\\subset P$ with $|p_1-p_2|=1$, and $U_{\\max}(n)=\\max_{|P|=n}U(P)$. The maximum exists since up to isometry the distance-$1$ graph depends continuously on $P$ and the count is integer-valued; in any case $\\max$ can be read as supremum over configurations, attained up to arbitrarily close perturbation.\\\\ We claim no exact formula, no $\\Theta$-tightness and no matching exponent. The provable best-known statement is: there exist absolute constants $c,C,c'>0$ and $n_0$ such that for all $n\\ge n_0$, $$c\\, n^{1+c'/\\log\\log n} \\le U_{\\max}(n) \\le C\\, n^{4/3}.$$ In particular $\\log\\log n$ is defined and $>0$ for $n$ large; we take $n_0\\ge 16$ so $\\log\\log n\\ge 1$. The upper bound is $O(n^{4/3})$ and the lower bound is superlinear of the stated $n^{1+\\Omega(1/\\log\\log n)}$ shape. The gap remains open.\\\\ Lemma (Monotonicity). $U_{\\max}(n)$ is nondecreasing in $n$. Indeed, adding a point cannot destroy existing unit pairs.\\\\ Proof. Let $P=\\{p_1,\\dots,p_n\\}$ achieve (or nearly achieve) $U_{\\max}(n)$. Let $M=\\max_i |p_i|$. Choose $q\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ with $|q|>M+2$, e.g. $q=(M+3,0)$. Then for every $i$, $|q-p_i|\\ge |q|-|p_i|>2>1$. Hence $q$ forms no distance-$1$ pair with $P$, and the pairs inside $P$ are unchanged. So with $P'=P\\cup\\{q\\}$, $|P'|=n+1$ and $U(P')=U(P)$. Therefore $U_{\\max}(n+1)\\ge U(P')=U(P)$. Taking $P$ optimal gives $U_{\\max}(n+1)\\ge U_{\\max}(n)$. More generally the union of finitely many unit circles $\\bigcup_{p\\in P}\\{x:|x-p|=1\\}$ has empty interior complement nonempty, so a generic far point avoids distance $1$. Iterating gives monotonicity.\\\\ Corollary (Padding from squares). To prove the lower bound it suffices to prove it for square cardinalities $m^2$. Precisely, suppose for all sufficiently large integers $m$, $$U_{\\max}(m^2)\\ge c_0\\,(m^2)^{1+c'/\\log\\log(m^2)}$$ for absolute $c_0,c'>0$. Then there exist $c>0$, $n_0$ such that for all $n\\ge n_0$, $U_{\\max}(n)\\ge c\\,n^{1+c'/\\log\\log n}$.\\\\ Proof. Let $n$ be large and $m=\\lfloor\\sqrt{n}\\rfloor$, so $m^2\\le n<(m+1)^2$ and $m\\to\\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty$. By monotonicity, $U_{\\max}(n)\\ge U_{\\max}(m^2)$.

