sing involves $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nPlanarize: replace each crossing by a new degree-$4$ vertex. Let $G^{*}$ have $V^{*}=v+\\mathrm{cr}$ vertices and $E^{*}=e+2\\mathrm{cr}$ edges, since each crossing splits two edges into four half-edges, net $+2$ edges per crossing. $G^{*}$ is planar by construction. We claim $G^{*}$ is simple under the goodness assumption when $G$ is simple. Indeed loops would require self-crossings, excluded. A parallel pair in $G^{*}$ would require either two original vertices joined by two original edges, excluded by simplicity of $G$, or two edges crossing twice consecutively, or adjacent edges crossing, both excluded. More precisely, two $G^{*}$-vertices can be consecutive along at most one edge-segment. Hence $G^{*}$ is simple loopless.\n\nIf $V^{*}\\ge 3$, which holds if $v\\ge 3$, Lemma 1 gives $E^{*}\\le 3V^{*}-6$ provided $G^{*}$ is connected; if disconnected the same bound $E^{*}\\le 3V^{*}-6$ holds by Lemma 1. Thus $e+2\\mathrm{cr}\\le 3(v+\\mathrm{cr})-6$, i.e. $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge e-3v+6$. For disconnected $G$ the same planarization argument applies globally, so connectivity of $G$ is not needed. This proves the linear bound. In particular for any induced drawing, $\\mathrm{cr}_p\\ge e_p-3v_p$ holds for all $v_p$: if $v_p\\ge 3$ this follows from $\\ge e_p-3v_p+6$, and if $v_p\\le 2$ then $e_p\\le 1$ by simplicity so $e_p-3v_p<0\\le \\mathrm{cr}_p$.\n\nLemma 3 (Crossing lemma, simple). If $G$", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "cr(G) >= e-3v+6 from Euler's bound", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "The candidate proof body after 'Candidate Proof Body (Writer 1):' is empty. There is no planarization argument deleting one edge per crossing, no application of E <= 3V-6 to t