  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a finite graph drawn in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with vertices as distinct points and edges as continuous curves joining their endpoints, with no edge passing through a third vertex except at endpoints. Define $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ as the minimum number of interior intersection points of distinct edges over all such drawings, where intersections are counted with the usual general-position requirement that no three edges meet in one interior point and no two meet tangentially. We prove the crossing lemma for simple graphs and its transfer to multiplicity $\\le 2$.\n\nAssume $G$ is simple, loopless, with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. Loops are absent in the unit-distance application since arcs join distinct consecutive points.\n\nLemma 1 (Euler). If $H$ is simple planar with $V\\ge 3$ then $E\\le 3V-6$, connected or not.\n\nIndeed for connected $H$ with $V\\ge 3$, Euler's $V-E+F=2$ and $3F\\le 2E$ since every face has at least $3$ edge-sides and each edge belongs to at most $2$ faces, giving $E\\le 3V-6$. If disconnected, connect components by adding edges between outer-face vertices, preserving simplicity and planarity until connected; the bound for the connected supergraph implies it for $H$. In particular components with $1$ or $2$ vertices satisfy $E_i\\le V_i-1$, which is even stronger, so summation also gives $E\\le 3V-6$ when $V\\ge 3$.\n\nLemma 2 (Linear bound). If $G$ is simple with $v\\ge 3$ then $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6$.\n\nProof. Take an optimal drawing achieving $\\mathrm{cr}=\\mathrm{cr}(G)$. We may assume it is good: no edge crosses itself, two edges sharing an endpoint do not cross, and two edges cross at most once. Indeed if adjacent edges cross, swapping the initial segments at the crossing reduces the crossing count; if two edges cross twice, swapping middle segments reduces crossings by at least $2$. Also perturb to avoid three concurrent crossings and tangencies and edge-vertex incidences. Hence in an optimal drawing every crossing involves $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nPlanarize: replace each crossing by a new degree-$4$ vertex. Let $G^{*}$ have $V^{*}=v+\\mathrm{cr}$ vertices and $E^{*}=e+2\\mathrm{cr}$ edges, since each crossing splits two edges into four half-edges, net $+2$ edges per crossing. $G^{*}$ is planar by construction. We claim $G^{*}$ is simple under the goodness assumption when $G$ is simple. Indeed loops would require self-crossings, excluded. A parallel pair in $G^{*}$ would require either two original vertices joined by two original edges, excluded by simplicity of $G$, or two edges crossing twice consecutively, or adjacent edges crossing, both excluded. More precisely, two $G^{*}$-vertices can be consecutive along at most one edge-segment. Hence $G^{*}$ is simple loopless.\n\nIf $V^{*}\\ge 3$, which holds if $v\\ge 3$, Lemma 1 gives $E^{*}\\le 3V^{*}-6$ provided $G^{*}$ is connected; if disconnected the same bound $E^{*}\\le 3V^{*}-6$ holds by Lemma 1. Thus $e+2\\mathrm{cr}\\le 3(v+\\mathrm{cr})-6$, i.e. $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge e-3v+6$. For disconnected $G$ the same planarization argument applies globally, so connectivity of $G$ is not needed. This proves the linear bound. In particular for any induced drawing, $\\mathrm{cr}_p\\ge e_p-3v_p$ holds for all $v_p$: if $v_p\\ge 3$ this follows from $\\ge e_p-3v_p+6$, and if $v_p\\le 2$ then $e_p\\le 1$ by simplicity so $e_p-3v_p<0\\le \\mathrm{cr}_p$.\n\nLemma 3 (Crossing lemma, simple). If $G$", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "cr(G) >= e-3v+6 from Euler's bound", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "The candidate proof body after 'Candidate Proof Body (Writer 1):' is empty. There is no planarization argument deleting one edge per crossing, no application of E <= 3V-6 to t