  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. A drawing is a representation with distinct points for vertices and continuous arcs for edges meeting vertices only at endpoints. The crossing number $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is the minimum number of interior intersection points of edge-arcs over all such drawings, counted with multiplicity, under general position. We allow only transverse crossings. Non-optimal absolute constants are allowed.\n\nAssume known: if $H$ is simple planar with $|V(H)|\\ge 3$ and connected then $|E(H)|\\le 3|V(H)|-6$. Hence the same holds if disconnected and $|V|\\ge 3$: indeed join $k$ components by $k-1$ new edges preserving simplicity and planarity to get connected $H''$, so $|E|+k-1\\le 3|V|-6$, whence $|E|\\le 3|V|-6$.\n\nLemma (good drawing). There is an optimal drawing with $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ crossings such that: (i) no edge passes through a vertex except its endpoints, (ii) no three edges meet in one interior point, (iii) two edges sharing an endpoint do not cross, (iv) two edges cross at most once. In particular each crossing involves $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nIndeed, (i)-(ii) are by small perturbation. If two adjacent edges cross, swapping their tails near the crossing removes that crossing without increasing others. If two edges cross twice, swapping middle segments reduces crossing count by $2$. So a minimal drawing must satisfy (iii)-(iv).\n\nProposition 1. If $G$ is simple with $v\\ge 3$ then $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6$.\n\nProof. Fix a good optimal drawing with $X=\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ crossings. Each crossing involves $4$ distinct vertices by Lemma. Planarize: replace each crossing by a new dummy vertex of degree $4$. We obtain a planar graph $G'$ with $v'=v+X$ vertices and $e'=e+2X$ edges: each crossing splits two edges, adding $2$ edges net. By goodness, $G'$ has no loops and no parallel pair: a loop would come from original loop (absent as $G$ simple); a $2$-cycle would require either two adjacent original edges crossing (excluded) or the same pair crossing twice (excluded) or two segments of the same edge forming a cycle (impossible as consecutive dummies on one edge are distinct). More generally after small perturbation $G'$ can be assumed simple. Since $v\\ge 3$, $v'\\ge 3$. If $G'$ is disconnected, the remark above still gives $e'\\le 3v'-6$. Hence $e+2X\\le 3(v+X)-6$, i.e. $X\\ge e-3v+6$. This handles disconnected $G$, as the planarization argument does not require $G$ connected. Loops are absent in the application. $\\square$\n\nCorollary. For any simple $G$ (any $v$), $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v$. Indeed if $v\\ge 3$ this follows from Prop.\\ 1 since $+6\\ge 0$. If $v\\le 1$, $e=0$ (simple, no loops) so $e-3v\\le 0\\le\\mathrm{cr}$. If $v=2$, $e\\le 1$ so $e-3v\\le -5\\le\\mathrm{cr}$. Thus the weakened bound holds universally.\n\nProposition 2 (Crossing lemma). If $G$ is simple with $e\\ge 4v$ then $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e^3/(64v^2)$.\n\nProof", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "The section proves cr(G) >= e-3v+6 from Euler's bound.", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "The Candidate Proof Body is empty. No planarization argument is given: there is no deletion of one edge per crossing, no application of E <= 3V-6 to the remaining planar graph, and no handling of V<3 or disconnected graphs.", "cheap_test": "Inspect the proof body after 'Candidate Proof Body (Writer 2):'; it contains no text, equations, or planarization step."}, {"id": "O2", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "The section proves cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v by random induced subgraphs.", "quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "exact_quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) w