  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph drawn in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with vertices as points and edges as continuous arcs joining endpoints. The crossing number $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is the minimum over all drawings of the number of interior intersection points of pairs of edges, counted with multiplicity, where no three edges meet in one interior point after small perturbation. We prove the crossing lemma.\n\nAssume first $G$ simple. Fix a drawing attaining $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$. By standard normalization we may assume no edge crosses itself, no two adjacent edges cross except at their common endpoint, and no three edges are concurrent in one interior point; otherwise a local rerouting strictly reduces crossings. Hence each crossing involves exactly $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nLet $v$ be number of vertices, $e$ number of edges. For each crossing delete one of its two edges. After deleting at most $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges (one per crossing) the remaining drawing is planar. Thus there is a planar simple graph $G'$ on the same $v$ vertices with $e'\\ge e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges.\n\nWe use Euler's bound: every simple planar connected graph with $V\\ge 3$ satisfies $E\\le 3V-6$. For disconnected planar simple graphs the same bound holds when $v\\ge 3$. Indeed let components have $v_i,e_i$. If $v_i\\ge 3$ then $e_i\\le 3v_i-6$ by the connected case. If $v_i\\le 2$ then $e_i\\le v_i-1$ by simplicity ($0$ if $v_i=1$, $\\le 1$ if $v_i=2$). If at least one $v_i\\ge 3$, say $k\\ge 1$ such components, then $e\\le 3V_{\\mathrm{big}}-6k+V_{\\mathrm{small}}-l\\le 3v-6$ since $2V_{\\mathrm{small}}+6k+l\\ge 6$. If all $v_i\\le 2$ then $e\\le v-c\\le v-1\\le 3v-6$ for $v\\ge 3$. Hence for $v\\ge 3$, $e'\\le 3v-6$.\n\nConsequently for simple $G$ with $v\\ge 3$,\n$$e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\le e'\\le 3v-6,$$\nso\n$$\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6.$$\nIn particular $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v$. The latter inequality holds for all $v$, including $v<3$: if $v\\le 2$ and $G$ simple then $e\\le 1$ and $\\mathrm{cr}(G)=0