ng achieving cr=cr(G). Planarize: replace each crossing by a new dummy vertex of degree 4. Let G* have v*=v+cr vertices and e*=e+2cr edges (each crossing replaces 2 edges by 4, net +2). We claim G* is simple if the drawing was good and optimal. No loops: original has none and no edge self-crosses, subdivision creates no loop. No parallel pair: two original vertices cannot be doubly joined since G is simple and subdivision only removes adjacencies; an original vertex u and dummy x cannot be doubly joined, for x comes from crossing of e1,e2, and u can be incident to at most one of the four half-edges at x unless e1,e2 share u, in which case they would be adjacent and would not cross by goodness; two dummies x,y cannot be doubly joined, for that would require the same two original edges to cross at both x and y, contradicting (iii). Thus G* is simple planar. Moreover v*>=v>=3. If G* is disconnected the extended Euler bound still gives e*<=3v*-6. Hence e+2cr<=3(v+cr)-6, i.e. cr>=e-3v+6. This handles disconnected G as well, since the argument uses only the drawing of G.\n\nIn particular for any graph (induced subgraph) with v_p vertices, cr_p>=e_p-3v_p holds universally: if v_p>=3 this follows from Lemma 1 dropping +6; if v_p=0,1 then e_p=0 and e_p-3v_p<=0<=cr_p; if v_p=2 then simplicity gives e_p<=1 so e_p-3v_p<=-5<=cr_p.\n\nLemma 2 (crossing lemma). If G is simple with v vertices, e>=4v, then cr(G)>=e^3/(64v^2).\n\nProof. For v<3 the hypothesis e>=4v forces e<=1 (simple), impossible except v=0, so assume v>=3; the universal inequality above covers all random outcomes anyway. Choose p in (0,1] to be fixed later. Form random induced subgraph G", "solution_body": ""}, "analysis": null, "danus_fingerprint": "f233c51b46bbebf2", "is_stale": false, "is_invalidated": false, "rollouts": [{"raw": "{
