  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. By $cr(G)$ we mean the crossing number, the minimum over all drawings in the plane with distinct vertex points and edges as continuous curves meeting vertices only at endpoints, of the number of interior pairwise edge crossings. We may assume drawings are in general position: no three edges meet in one interior point, no vertex lies in the interior of an edge, and each crossing involves exactly two edges. For a crossing-minimal drawing we may further assume no two adjacent edges cross except at their common endpoint. Indeed if two edges $ab$ and $ac$ cross at an interior point $x$, swapping the segments from $x$ to $b$ and $x$ to $c$ removes that crossing and does not increase the total number of crossings. Hence in an optimal drawing every crossing uses $4$ distinct endpoints.\n\nAssume Euler's bound: every simple planar loopless graph with $v\\ge 3$ satisfies $e\\le 3v-6$. This holds also for disconnected graphs. Indeed for a connected component with $v_i\\ge 3$, $e_i\\le 3v_i-6$ by $V-E+F=2$ and $3F\\le 2E$. If $v_i=1$ then $e_i=0$; if $v_i=2$ then $e_i\\le 1$ by simplicity. Summing, let $k$ be the number of components with $v_i\\ge 3$, $t_2$ the number with $v_i=2$, $t_1$ with $v_i=1$. Then $e\\le \\sum_{v_i\\ge 3}(3v_i-6)+t_2 =3v-3(2t_2+t_1)-6k+t_2 =3v-5t_2-3t_1-6k$. If $k\\ge 1$ this is $\\le 3v-6$. If $k=0$ then $e\\le t_2\\le v/2\\le 3v-6$ for $v\\ge 3$. Hence the bound holds in general for $v\\ge 3$.\n\nLinear bound. Let $G$ be simple, $v\\ge 3$, drawn with $X$ crossings in optimal position. Delete one edge from each crossing pair. The resulting graph $G'$ with the restricted drawing has no crossings, hence is planar, has the same $v$ vertices and $e'\\ge e-X$ edges (deleting one edge per crossing removes at least all crossings; concurrent crossings only help). By simplicity $G'$ is still simple, so if $v\\ge 3$, $e'\\le 3v-6$. Hence $e-X\\le e'\\le 3v-6$, i.e.\n$$X\\ge e-3v+6.$$\nSince this holds for the optimal drawing, $cr(G)\\ge e-3v+6$. In particular $cr(G)\\ge e-3v$.\n\nIn fact for any induced subgraph the inequality $X'\\ge e'-3v'$ holds universally. Indeed if $v'\\ge 3$ it follows from the above since $X", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Entire Section 3 crossing-lemma proof", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "The Candidate Proof Body is empty. No drawing is fixed, no edge-deletion/planarization argument is given, and cr(G) >= e-3v+6 is not derived from E <= 3V-6. In particular the required step deleting one edge per crossing to obtain a planar simple graph with e-cr(G) edges, and the V>=3 hypothesis, are never used.", "cheap_test": "Search the proof body for any deletion of crossed edges, planar subgraph, or inequality e-cr(G) <= 3v-6; none is present."}, {"id": "O2", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Probabilistic bound cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "exact_quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "why": "No random induced subgraph is defined, no sampling probability p is chosen, and no expectations E[v'], E[e'], E[cr'] or inequality E[cr'] >= E[e']-3E[v'] are computed. The constant 1/64 and the threshold e>=4v, needed to make p=4v/e <= 1 and to absorb the linear term, are therefore unjustified.", "cheap_test": "Take e=4v: the claimed proof must produce p=1 and a non-vacuous calculation; with no p or expectation present the derivation cannot be checked even in this boundary case."}, {"id": "O3", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Extension to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2", "quote": "Extend the consequence to multigraphs with multipli