  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. The crossing number $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is the minimum number of interior intersection points of edge-curves over drawings with distinct vertices, edges as continuous curves joining endpoints, no edge passing through a vertex except its endpoints, no two adjacent edges crossing in their interiors, no three edges concurrent in one interior point, and at most two edges through one crossing. Such an optimal drawing exists and any drawing can be perturbed to satisfy the adjacency and concurrency conditions without increasing crossings: if two adjacent edges cross, swapping segments removes that crossing; triple points can be separated. Hence each crossing is determined by $4$ distinct vertices and $2$ distinct edges.\n\nAssume Euler's bound: every simple planar connected graph with $V\\ge 3$ satisfies $E\\le 3V-6$. We extend to disconnected. Let $G$ be simple planar, $v\\ge 3$, with components $G_i$, $v_i$ vertices, $e_i$ edges. If $v_i=1$, $e_i=0$. If $v_i=2$, $e_i\\le 1$. If $v_i\\ge 3$, $e_i\\le 3v_i-6$ by hypothesis. If no $v_i\\ge 3$, then $e\\le v/2\\le 3v-6$ for $v\\ge 3$. If at least one $v_i\\ge 3$, write $L$ for number of large components. Then $e\\le \\sum_{large}(3v_i-6)+\\sum_{v_i=2}1 \\le 3v-6L-5S_2-3S_1\\le 3v-6$ where $S_2,S_1$ count $2$-vertex and $1$-vertex components, since $1\\le 3\\cdot 2-5$. Thus any simple planar $G$ with $v\\ge 3$ satisfies $e\\le 3v-6$ even if disconnected.\n\nLinear bound: Let $G$ be simple with $v\\ge 3$, $e$ edges, drawn with $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ crossings in optimal position. For each crossing delete one of the two participating edges. After deleting at most $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges we obtain a subgraph $H$ with at least $e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges and a crossing-free drawing, hence planar. If $H$ is still with $v\\ge 3$, $e(H)\\le 3v-6$, so $e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\le 3v-6$, i.e. $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6$. In particular $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v$. For $v<3$ simple $e\\le 1$ and $e-3v\\le 0\\le \\mathrm{cr}(G)$, so $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge e-3v$ holds for all $v$ with simple $G$. This handles small $v$ and disconnected $H$.\n\nCubic bound by random sampling: Let $G$ be simple with $e\\ge 4v$ (hence $v\\ge 1$; if $v<3$ this cannot happen since $e\\le 1$). Choose $p=4v/e\\le 1$. Form random induced subgraph $G'$ by keeping each vertex independently with probability $p$, and all induced edges. Let $v',e',\\mathrm{cr}'$ be numbers of vertices, edges and crossings of $G'$ in the inherited drawing from an optimal drawing of $G$. Then $\\mathbb{E}[v']=pv$, $\\mathbb{E}[e']=p^2e$ since an edge survives iff its two endpoints survive, and $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{cr}']=p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ since each crossing involves $4$ distinct vertices by optimal position, all of which must survive. For every outcome, $\\mathrm{cr}'\\ge e'-3v'$ by the linear bound in its weak form (which is trivial when $v'<3$ as shown). Taking expectations, $p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge p^2e-3pv$. Dividing by $p^4$, $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e/p^2-3v/p^3$. With $p=4v/e$, $e/p^2=e^3/(16v^2)$ and $3v/p", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Euler planarization bound cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "The Candidate Proof Body is empty: no planarization argument is given. In particular there is no deletion of one edge per crossing, no application of E <= 3V-6 to the remaining planar simple graph, and no handling of V<3, disconnected graphs, or components where the Euler bound does not apply in the stated form.", "cheap_test": "Check the submitted body for any planar subgraph, crossing deletion, or Euler invocation; none is present. Test the missing edge cases v=1,2 and a disconnected graph."}, {"id": "O2", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Random induced subgraph bound cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "exact_quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "why": "No random induced subgraph is defined, no sampling probability p is chosen, and no expectations E[v'], E[e'], E[cr'] or inequality E[cr'] >= E[e']-3E[v'] are computed. The constant 64 and the threshold e>=4v are therefore not derived.", "cheap_test": "Search the body for a probability p, e.g. p=4v/e, and for expectation calculations; none is present."}, {"id": "O3", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Extension to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2", "quote": "Extend the consequence to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2 by passing to a simple subgraph with at least half the edges", "exact_quote": "Extend the consequence to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2 by passing to a simple subgraph with at least half the edges", "why": "No simple subgraph is constructed, no proof that at least half the edges survive deletion of parallel duplicates is given, and no argument is given that crossings of the multigraph are at least those of the subgraph or how the resulting constant changes. The claimed extension is asserted in the statement but never proved.", "cheap_test": "Take a multigraph where every edge is doubled; verify the body contains no deduplication step or factor-2 loss calculation; it contains none."}, {"id": "O4", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Boundary cases: disconnected, small v, loops, e<4v versus e>=4v", "quote": "handling disconnected graphs, small v, loops (absent in the application), and the split e<4v versus e>=4v", "exact_quote": "handling disconnected graphs, small v, loops (absent in the application), and the split e<4v versus e>=4v", "why": "None of these cases is addressed. Without the e<4v split the cubic bound is not usable as stated for sparse graphs, without small-v handling Euler E <= 3V-6 for V>=3 does not cover the application, and without excluding loops the simple-graph reduction fails. An empty body cannot discharge these obligations.", "cheap_test": "Apply the unproved claim to v<3, to a graph with a loop, and to e<4v; no case analysis in the body distinguishes them."}]}}, {"raw": "{
