  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph drawn in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with vertices as distinct points and edges as continuous curves joining their endpoints, with no edge passing through a vertex except its endpoints. The crossing number $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is the minimum number of interior intersection points of pairs of edges over all such drawings, where in an optimal drawing we may assume goodness: no two adjacent edges cross, no edge crosses itself, no two edges cross more than once, and no three edges are concurrent in an interior point. Indeed any adjacent crossing or self-crossing can be removed by rerouting without increasing crossings, and a pair crossing twice encloses a lens whose swapping does not increase total crossings. Hence each crossing involves $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nAssume as black box Euler's formula: a connected simple planar graph with $V\\ge 3$ satisfies $E\\le 3V-6$. This follows from $V-E+F=2$ and $2E\\ge 3F$ since each face has at least $3$ edges. We extend to possibly disconnected simple planar graphs.\n\nLemma (planar edge bound). If $G'$ is simple planar loopless with $v\\ge 3$ vertices (possibly disconnected) then $e'\\le 3v-6$.\n\nProof. Let $c$ be the number of connected components. If $c=1$ this is the cited bound. If $c>1$, take a planar embedding. Add $c-1$ edges between distinct components to make it connected: inductively join two components by a curve in a common face (e.g. outer face) joining a vertex of one to a vertex of the other; since endpoints lie in different components the new edge is not already present, so simplicity is preserved, and planarity is preserved. The resulting connected simple planar graph has $v$ vertices and $e'+c-1$ edges, so $e'+c-1\\le 3v-6$. Hence $e'\\le 3v-6-c+1\\le 3v-6$. Components with $1$ or $2$ vertices contribute $0$ or at most $1$ edge, compatible with this. $\\square$\n\nProposition (linear crossing bound). Let $G$ be simple with $v\\ge 3$ vertices and $e$ edges. Then $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6$.\n\nProof. Take an optimal good drawing with exactly $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ crossings. Delete one edge from each crossing until no crossing remains. Since deleting one of the two edges at a crossing destroys that crossing (and possibly others), at most $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges are deleted. We obtain a planar simple spanning subgraph $G'$ with $v$ vertices and $e'\\ge e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges. If $e'\\le 1$ the claimed inequality is trivial or follows below; otherwise if $v\\ge 3$, by the Lemma $e'\\le 3v-6$. Hence $e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\le e'\\le 3v-6$, i.e. $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6$. More precisely if $e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is negative the bound is still valid since $e-3v+6$ may be positive only when $e$ large. Formally $e'\\ge e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ and $e'\\le 3v-6$ give the result. $\\square$\n\nIn particular for any simple $G$, $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v$. Indeed if $v\\ge 3$ this follows from $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge e-3v+6\\ge e-3v$. If $v\\le 2$, simplicity and looplessness imply $e\\le 1$: $v=0$ gives $e=0$, $v=1$ gives $e=0$, $v=2$ gives $e\\le 1$. Then $e-3v\\le 0$ except $v=0,e=0$ where it is $0$, while $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge 0$. So $\\mathrm{cr}\\ge e-3v$ holds universally for simple loopless $G$.\n\nTheorem (crossing lemma, simple case). Let $G$ be simple with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. If $e\\ge 4v$ then $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e^3/(64v^2)$. Constants are non-optimal.\n\nProof. Assume $e\\ge 4v$ so $p:=4v/e\\le 1$. Form a random induced subgraph $G_p$ by keeping each vertex independently with probability $p$. Let $v_p,e_p,\\mathrm{cr}_p$ be the numbers of vertices, edges, and crossings of the induced drawing (restriction of the optimal drawing of $G$). Then $\\mathbb{E}[v_p]=pv$, $\\mathbb{E}[e_p]=p^2e$ since an edge survives iff its two distinct endpoints survive. Moreover $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{cr}_p]=p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)$. Indeed fix an optimal good drawing of $G$; each crossing is defined by two edges with $4$ distinct endpoints (adjacent edges do not cross in optimum), so it survives iff all $4$ vertices survive, with probability $p^4$, and linearity of expectation gives the claim. No triple concurrency is assumed by goodness, so counting is exact.\n\nFor every realization, $\\mathrm{cr}_p\\ge e_p-3v_p$ by the universal weak linear bound above. Taking expectations, $p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{cr}_p]\\ge \\mathbb{E}[e_p]-3\\mathbb{E}[v_p]=p^2e-3pv$. Dividing by $p^4$, $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e/p^2-3v/p^3$. Substituting $p=4v/e$ gives $e/p^2=e^3/(16v^2)$ and $3v/p^3=3e^3/(64v^2)$, whose difference is $e^3/(64v^2)$. Hence $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e^3/(64v^2)$. $\\square$\n\nCorollary (multigraph with multiplicity $\\le 2$). Let $G$ be loopless multigraph with $v$ vertices, $e$ edges, max multiplicity at most $2$. Then with $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ defined as minimum crossings (parallel edges allowed to be coincident only after perturbation; we count transverse crossings), $e\\le 8v+8v^{2/3}\\mathrm{cr}(G)^{1/3}$. In particular $e=O(v+v^{2/3}\\mathrm{cr}(G)^{1/3})$.\n\nProof. Loops are absent in the application (arcs join distinct points); in general loops can be deleted losing at most one edge per loop without increasing crossings, so assume loopless. Obtain a simple spanning subgraph $G'$ by keeping exactly one edge from each parallel pair. Then $e'\\ge e/2$ since multiplicity $\\le 2$. Any drawing of $G$ restricts to a drawing of $G'$ with at most as many crossings, so $\\mathrm{cr}(G')\\le \\mathrm{cr}(G)$.\n\nIf $v\\le 2$, then $e'\\le 1$ so $e\\le 2$, and the claimed bound holds trivially. Assume $v\\ge 3.\n\nSplit into two cases. If $e'<4v$ then $e\\le 2e'<8v$. If $e'\\ge 4v$ then by the Theorem applied to simple $G'$, $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge \\mathrm{cr}(G')\\ge e'^3/(64v^2)\\ge (e/2)^3/(64v^2)=e^3/(512v^2)$. Hence $e^3\\le 512v^2\\mathrm{cr}(G)$, i.e. $e\\le 8v^{2/3}\\mathrm{cr}(G)^{1/3}$ since", "falsifier_critique": {"verdict": "reject", "objections": [{"id": "O1", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Entire Section 3 proof: the candidate proof body is empty, so no derivation is provided.", "quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "exact_quote": "Prove from Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 for simple planar graphs with V>=3 that cr(G) >= e-3v+6", "why": "There is no proof text after the heading 'Candidate Proof Body (Writer 3):'. In particular there is no planarization argument deleting one edge per crossing, no application of E <= 3V-6 to the remaining planar graph, and no handling of V<3, disconnected graphs, or components. The claimed inequality is therefore asserted, not proved.", "cheap_test": "Inspect the candidate body between 'Candidate Proof Body (Writer 3):' and 'Audit this section derivation.'; it contains no mathematical steps, definitions, or equations."}, {"id": "O2", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Random-induced-subgraph crossing bound cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) for e>=4v.", "quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "exact_quote": "by random induced subgraphs that cr(G) >= e^3/(64v^2) when e>=4v", "why": "No sampling probability p, no expected vertex/edge/crossing counts, no inequality E[cr] >= E[e']-3E[v'], no optimization p=4v/e, and no use of the hypothesis e>=4v to ensure p<=1 are given. The constant 64 is not derived.", "cheap_test": "Search the candidate body for a probability p, expectation, or the choice p=4v/e; none appears because the body is empty."}, {"id": "O3", "severity": "critical", "claim_attacked": "Extension to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2 via a simple subgraph with at least half the edges.", "quote": "Extend the consequence to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2 by passing to a simple subgraph with at least half the edges", "exact_quote": "Extend the consequence to multigraphs with multiplicity at most 2 by passing to a simple subgraph with at least half the edges", "why": "No construction of the simple subgraph, no proof it retains at least e/2 edges, no monotonicity statement that crossing number does not increase under taking subgraphs, and no resulting loss of constant are provided. This extension is essential for the arc-graph application with multiplicity at most 2.", "cheap_test": "Search the candidate body for 'multiplicity', 'parallel', or a subgraph-retention argument; none appears."}, {"id": "O4", "severity": "major", "claim_attacked": "Boundary and case handling: disconnected graphs, small v, absent loops, and the split e<4v versus e>=4v.", "quote": "handling disconnected graphs, small v, loops (absent in the application), and the split e<4v versus e>=4v", "exact_quote": "handling disconnected graphs, small v, loops (absent in the application), and the split e<4v versus e>=4v", "why": "None of these cases is treated. Euler's bound E <= 3V-6 requires care for V<3 and disconnected planar graphs, loops would invalidate the simple-graph Euler application, and the e<4v case must be separated because the sampling probability and the cubic bound argument differ. An empty body cannot discharge these obligations.", "cheap_test": "Check the candidate body for any case split on v<3, disconnected components, loops, or e<4v versus e>=4v; no such text exists."}]}}, {"raw": "{
  "status": "solved",
  "solution_body": "Let $G$ be a graph drawn in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ with vertices as points and edges as continuous arcs joining endpoints. The crossing number $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ is the minimum over all drawings of the number of interior intersection points of pairs of edges, counted with multiplicity, where no three edges meet in one interior point after small perturbation. We prove the crossing lemma.\n\nAssume first $G$ simple. Fix a drawing attaining $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$. By standard normalization we may assume no edge crosses itself, no two adjacent edges cross except at their common endpoint, and no three edges are concurrent in one interior point; otherwise a local rerouting strictly reduces crossings. Hence each crossing involves exactly $4$ distinct vertices.\n\nLet $v$ be number of vertices, $e$ number of edges. For each crossing delete one of its two edges. After deleting at most $\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges (one per crossing) the remaining drawing is planar. Thus there is a planar simple graph $G'$ on the same $v$ vertices with $e'\\ge e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)$ edges.\n\nWe use Euler's bound: every simple planar connected graph with $V\\ge 3$ satisfies $E\\le 3V-6$. For disconnected planar simple graphs the same bound holds when $v\\ge 3$. Indeed let components have $v_i,e_i$. If $v_i\\ge 3$ then $e_i\\le 3v_i-6$ by the connected case. If $v_i\\le 2$ then $e_i\\le v_i-1$ by simplicity ($0$ if $v_i=1$, $\\le 1$ if $v_i=2$). If at least one $v_i\\ge 3$, say $k\\ge 1$ such components, then $e\\le 3V_{\\mathrm{big}}-6k+V_{\\mathrm{small}}-l\\le 3v-6$ since $2V_{\\mathrm{small}}+6k+l\\ge 6$. If all $v_i\\le 2$ then $e\\le v-c\\le v-1\\le 3v-6$ for $v\\ge 3$. Hence for $v\\ge 3$, $e'\\le 3v-6$.\n\nConsequently for simple $G$ with $v\\ge 3$,\n$$e-\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\le e'\\le 3v-6,$$\nso\n$$\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v+6.$$\nIn particular $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge e-3v$. The latter inequality holds for all $v$, including $v<3$: if $v\\le 2$ and $G$ simple then $e\\le 1$ and $\\mathrm{cr}(G)=0\\ge e-3v$, since $e-3v\\le 1-6<0$ for $v=2$, etc. For $v_p\\ge 3$ the $+6$ version implies the weaker version; for $v_p<3$ it is checked directly. This universal weak form will be used for random subgraphs.\n\nNow suppose $e\\ge 4v$. Then $v\\ge 9$ for simple $G$ (since $e\\le v(v-1)/2$), in particular $v\\ge 3$. Choose $p=4v/e\\le 1$. Form a random induced subgraph $G_p$ by keeping each vertex independently with probability $p$. Let $v_p,e_p$ be its numbers of vertices and edges, and $\\mathrm{cr}_p$ the number of crossings of the induced drawing inherited from the optimal drawing of $G$. Then\n$$\\mathbb{E}[v_p]=pv,\\quad \\mathbb{E}[e_p]=p^2e,$$\nsince an edge survives iff both endpoints survive. Moreover $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathrm{cr}_p]=p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)$, since each crossing uses $4$ distinct vertices by normalization and survives iff all $4$ survive.\n\nFor every outcome, $\\mathrm{cr}_p\\ge e_p-3v_p$ by the discussion above (using $+6$ bound when $v_p\\ge 3$ and trivial check when $v_p<3$). Taking expectations,\n$$p^4\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge p^2e-3pv.$$\nDividing by $p^4$,\n$$\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge \\frac{e}{p^2}-\\frac{3v}{p^3}.$$\nWith $p=4v/e$, $e/p^2=e^3/(16v^2)$ and $3v/p^3=3e^3/(64v^2)$, so\n$$\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge \\frac{e^3}{16v^2}-\\frac{3e^3}{64v^2}=\\frac{e^3}{64v^2}.$$\nThis is the crossing lemma for simple graphs when $e\\ge 4v$, with non-optimal constant $1/64$ allowed.\n\nExtension to multiplicity at most $2$. Let $G$ be a multigraph with no loops and edge-multiplicity $\\mu\\le 2$, with $v$ vertices and $e$ edges. Loops are absent in the unit-distance arc-graph application. Obtain a simple subgraph $G'$ by keeping exactly one edge from each parallel pair. Then $G'$ is simple, has $v$ vertices and $e'\\ge e/2$ edges. Deleting edges cannot increase crossing number, so $\\mathrm{cr}(G)\\ge \\mathrm{cr}(G')$ (restrict an optimal drawing of $G$ to $G'$).\n\nIf $v<3$ then with no loops and $\\mu\\le 2$, $e\\le 4$ ($v=1$: $e=0$; $v=2$: $e\\le 2$), so $e=O(v+1)$ 